cards: [
{h:'ACM Code of Ethics (2018): all 4 sections & Principle 2.9',b:`<p><b>ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct</b> (adopted June 22, 2018; Task Force chair Don Gotterbarn; adapted by IFIP). Preamble: <i>"The public good is always the primary consideration."</i> The Code is not an algorithm for solving ethical problems, but a basis for ethical decision-making and remediation.</p><ul><li><b>Section 1 — General Ethical Principles (1.1–1.7):</b> <b>1.1</b> Contribute to society and human well-being, acknowledging all people are stakeholders (prioritize the less advantaged; protect autonomy and environment) · <b>1.2</b> Avoid harm (report signs of system risk; <b>blow the whistle</b> if leaders do not act) · <b>1.3</b> Be honest and trustworthy (disclose limitations and conflicts of interest) · <b>1.4</b> Be fair and take action not to discriminate · <b>1.5</b> Respect the work required to produce new ideas, inventions, creative works and computing artifacts (credit creators; respect copyright, patents, trade secrets, licences; support FOSS) · <b>1.6</b> Respect privacy (only minimum necessary data; prevent re-identification when merging collections) · <b>1.7</b> Honor confidentiality (except when there is evidence of a violation of law, regulations or the Code).</li><li><b>Section 2 — Professional Responsibilities (2.1–2.9):</b> <b>2.1</b> Strive for high quality · <b>2.2</b> Maintain competence, conduct &amp; ethical practice · <b>2.3</b> Know and respect existing rules (challenge unethical rules through channels before violating and accepting consequences) · <b>2.4</b> Accept and provide professional review · <b>2.5</b> Give comprehensive, thorough risk evaluations (extraordinary care for <b>machine learning systems</b>; if future risks cannot be reliably predicted, reassess frequently or <b>do not deploy</b>) · <b>2.6</b> Work only in areas of competence · <b>2.7</b> Foster public awareness · <b>2.8</b> Access resources only when authorized or compelled by the public good · <b>2.9 Design and implement systems that are robustly and usably secure</b> (<i>Section A Q.x exact quote:</i> <b>"To ensure the system achieves its intended purpose, security features should be designed to be as intuitive and easy to use as possible."</b> If misuse/harm is unavoidable, the best option may be to <b>not implement the system</b>).</li><li><b>Section 3 — Professional Leadership Principles (3.1–3.7):</b> <b>3.1</b> Public good central across all lifecycle phases · <b>3.2</b> Social responsibilities · <b>3.3</b> Enhance quality of working life (physical safety, psychological well-being, ergonomics) · <b>3.4</b> Policies reflecting the Code · <b>3.5</b> Growth opportunities · <b>3.6</b> Use care when <b>modifying or retiring legacy systems</b> (assist graceful migration) · <b>3.7</b> Special stewardship for systems integrated into <b>society's infrastructure</b>.</li><li><b>Section 4 — Compliance (4.1–4.2):</b> <b>4.1</b> Uphold, promote and respect the Code · <b>4.2</b> Treat violations as inconsistent with ACM membership.</li></ul>`},
{h:'BCS Code of Conduct (2022) & professional associations on the slides',b:`<p><b>Five associations on the Codes slides:</b> (1) <b>BCS</b> (British Computer Society, Chartered Institute for IT: 57,625 members as of 2021; Royal Charter 1984) · (2) <b>ACM</b> (founded 1947, HQ New York City, ~110,000 members as of 2022, motto: <i>"Advancing Computing as a Science &amp; Profession"</i>) · (3) <b>IEEE Computer Society</b> (45+ PD hours, 225 conferences, 350+ chapters, 40 technical communities) · (4) <b>Nigerian Computer Society (NCS)</b> · (5) <b>Computer Professionals Registration Council of Nigeria (CPN)</b>.</p><p><b>BCS Code of Conduct (Version 8, 8 June 2022) — 4 Pillars:</b></p><ol><li><b>1. Public Interest:</b> due regard for public health, privacy, security, wellbeing and environment; legitimate rights of <b>Third Parties</b> (anyone affected, directly aware or not, including data protection &amp; IP); non-discrimination; equal access &amp; social inclusion.</li><li><b>2. Professional Competence and Integrity:</b> work only within competence; never claim competence you lack; continuing professional development (CPD); comply with Legislation; value alternative viewpoints and honest criticism; avoid injuring others by false, malicious or negligent action/inaction; reject bribery.</li><li><b>3. Duty to Relevant Authority</b> (employer/client, or university for students): due care and professional judgement; avoid conflicts of interest; accept responsibility for your work and supervisees; protect confidential info; never misrepresent product performance. <i>Appendix 1 rule:</i> when employer instructions conflict with professional judgement, <b>indicate the likely risks and consequences</b>.</li><li><b>4. Duty to the Profession:</b> uphold reputation of the profession and BCS; improve standards; support colleagues. <i>Appendix 1 rule:</i> leaders must ensure no one is penalised for raising concerns, and members must <b>support a colleague who in good faith blows the whistle</b> on danger, risk, malpractice or wrongdoing. Standards are <b>higher than general law</b>; members must report breaches immediately (disciplinary action up to expulsion).</li></ol>`},
{h:'8 group topics, benefits of joining & the 4 class dilemmas',b:`<p><b>Eight group-learning documents on Slide 3:</b> 1. ACM Code · 2. Cybercrime Act · 3. IEEE Code of Ethics · 4. Trademark Act (Nigeria) · 5. CPN Code of Conduct · 6. BCS Code of Conduct · 7. EFCC Act · 8. Nigerian Criminal Code.</p><p><b>Why join a professional association even if you have a CS degree? (Slides 9–12 &amp; Class Discussion Option 4):</b> shows career commitment on your CV (sets graduates with limited work experience apart); discounted student membership; networking, conferences and focus groups; journals, salary surveys and workplace agreement advice; scholarships/prizes; professional registration and overseas qualification assessment; mentoring and job listings; and crucial <b>peer support + an authoritative ethical code to back you when resisting unethical employer instructions</b>.</p><p><b>Four class discussion dilemmas:</b> (1) <b>Option 1 ("Just write the code"):</b> Nigerian fintech loan algorithm rejecting low-income communities before tomorrow's deadline (ACM 1.1, 1.2, 1.4, 2.5; BCS Public Interest &amp; stating risks to Relevant Authority); (2) <b>Option 2 ("I agree" campus app):</b> free student housing/transport app harvesting contacts, location and browsing for advertisers (NDPA 2023 data minimisation &amp; purpose limitation; Barocas &amp; Nissenbaum consent critique); (3) <b>Option 3 (Legal &amp; profitable facial recognition):</b> workplace/visitor monitoring less accurate for some demographics and covert (law ≠ ethics; ACM 1.4, 1.6, 2.5); (4) <b>Option 4 (Two job offers &amp; why join an association)</b>.</p>`}
],
