model: `<p><b>Background:</b> Section C is worth 10 marks (25 minutes, maximum one page). The full model answer for <b>Question 3 (Five Practical Learning Points)</b> is in <b>Topic 9 Theory</b>, and the full model answer for <b>Question 4 Option A (Four Ethical Concerns of Big Data)</b> is in <b>Topic 7 Theory</b>. Below is the companion one-page model for <b>Question 4 Option B (Four Main Ethical Concerns of Recommendation Engines)</b> with each concern clearly numbered and identifiable.</p><p><b>Answer and solution (Q4 Option B — Four Clearly Identifiable Ethical Concerns of Recommendation Engines):</b></p><ol><li><b>Concern 1 — Epistemic Bubbles, Echo Chambers and Societal Polarisation (Nguyen, 2020):</b> recommendation engines optimise engagement through selective algorithmic filtering (Pariser's filter bubble), omitting contrary viewpoints and creating <b>epistemic bubbles</b> where users mistake pre-filtered agreement for independent proof (<b>bootstrapped corroboration</b>). When algorithms amplify sensational content that discredits outside experts, bubbles harden into <b>echo chambers</b> where contrary facts trigger <b>disagreement-reinforcement</b>, threatening democratic discourse.</li><li><b>Concern 2 — Covert Online Manipulation, Addiction and Loss of Autonomy (Susser et al., 2019; EU Ethics by Design):</b> because digital screens recede from awareness through <b>mediation</b>, recommender systems act as dynamic, personalized choice architectures (<b>hypernudging</b>) that covertly target users' emotional and cognitive vulnerabilities (such as the leaked Facebook document on insecure teenagers). This hidden influence subverts conscious authorship (<b>autonomy</b>) and purposely stimulates compulsive attachment and anxiety (<b>Day 2 Case 4</b>), violating the EU Ethics by Design ban on addictive design.</li><li><b>Concern 3 — Invisible Programming Values and Opacity (Moor, 1985; Miller &amp; Record):</b> algorithms are never value-neutral. Choosing <i>"maximise watch time and click-through rate"</i> embeds <b>invisible programming values</b> (Moor) that subordinate user mental health and truth to advertising revenue, while secret, hyper-individualised filtering leaves users unable to inspect or contest why specific content is promoted or suppressed.</li><li><b>Concern 4 — Pervasive Behavioural Surveillance and Intimate Inference (Barocas &amp; Nissenbaum, 2014):</b> to power personalisation, recommender platforms harvest granular watch times, pauses, location and social graphs. This defeats traditional notice-and-consent (the <b>transparency paradox</b> and <b>unanticipated uses</b>) and allows platforms to infer intimate traits—such as political leanings, depression or financial distress—even about non-consenting users (<b>tyranny of the minority</b>).</li></ol><p><b>Scenario (Practical Solution &amp; Application):</b> to address all four concerns, a Nigerian news and video streaming app implements Value Sensitive Design under the <b>NDPA 2023</b>: it adds <i>"Why am I seeing this?"</i> transparency labels and a one-tap chronological/non-personalised feed toggle (fixing Concerns 2 &amp; 3), injects verified cross-perspective stories to prevent epistemic bubbles (fixing Concern 1), disables behavioural profiling for minors, and minimises data retention (fixing Concern 4).</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> behavioural surveillance (Concern 4) feeds opaque engagement-first models (Concern 3) that covertly manipulate individual attention (Concern 2) and fracture society into bubbles and echo chambers (Concern 1).</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> clearly numbering and defining these four concerns—and pairing them with transparency, user agency, epistemic diversity and NDPA data minimisation—delivers a complete, exam-ready Section C essay.</p>`
